
========================================================================
  STEP 0 — Launch the server
========================================================================
The client starts spec mcp serve as a child process and talks JSON-RPC 2.0 over stdin/stdout.
Command: /Users/davidparry/code/github/tdd-bdd-agentic/harness/target/debug/spec mcp serve --root /Users/davidparry/code/github/tdd-bdd-agentic

========================================================================
  STEP 1 — tools/list (discovery)
========================================================================
JSON-RPC request: method="tools/list" — the agent asks what it is allowed to do.
[boundedElastic-1] INFO io.modelcontextprotocol.client.transport.StdioClientTransport - MCP server starting.
[boundedElastic-1] INFO io.modelcontextprotocol.client.transport.StdioClientTransport - MCP server started
[pool-1-thread-1] INFO io.modelcontextprotocol.client.LifecycleInitializer - Server response with Protocol: 2025-11-25, Capabilities: ServerCapabilities[completions=null, experimental=null, logging=null, prompts=null, resources=null, tools=ToolCapabilities[listChanged=null]], Info: Implementation[name=spec-driven-server, title=null, version=1.0.0, description=null, icons=null, websiteUrl=null] and Instructions Drives a spec-driven TDD/BDD workflow. The requirements spec is the source of truth and the entry point. Spec iteration loop: draft or edit a requirement in the requirements file -> validate_spec until the spec is valid -> refine_requirement on the new or changed requirement and reword from its findings until there are none -> have the developer approve the wording. From an approved spec: list_requirements -> get_requirement (pick a pending one) -> write a Gherkin scenario from its acceptance criteria in the feature file (BDD level), add step definitions if needed, and/or a failing unit test -> run_tests (expect RED) -> implement (command_run can build or install what the failing tests need; it only works on a RED bar) -> run_tests (expect GREEN) -> start_refactor -> run_tests. The human developer stays in control of every engineering decision.
- changes_commit: Apply every staged change to the working tree and clear the area.
- changes_discard: Drop every staged change without applying it.
- changes_show: Show every staged change waiting for review.
- changes_validate: Validate the effective spec and staged Gherkin together — staged content wins over the working tree.
- command_run: Run one allowlisted dev-tool command (cargo, mvn, npm, npx, node, dotnet, java, javac, tsc) inside the project root during the implementation phase — the bar must be RED.
- feature_create: Create an empty feature file (staged; apply with changes_commit).
- feature_list: List every Gherkin feature file with its name and scenario count.
- feature_read: Read one parsed feature file: tags, scenarios, and steps.
- get_requirement: Get the user story and acceptance criteria for one requirement.
- get_tdd_state: Get the current phase of the Red/Green/Refactor cycle, the last test run summary, interpretation instructions, at most the three latest dated state entries, and a suggested next step.
- list_requirements: List every requirement of the kata with its id, title, and implementation status.
- project_inspect: Detect the project's languages, BDD frameworks, runtimes, and whether test execution is possible.
- refine_requirement: Review one requirement's wording for quality: ambiguous language, a story missing its actor or rationale, outcomes that are not measurable, criteria covering more than one action, and missing edge cases.
- requirement_mark_implemented: Flip a requirement's status to implemented (staged).
- run_tests: Run the project test suite and report the outcome.
- scenario_add: Append a scenario tagged @REQ-...
- scenario_delete: Remove a scenario from a feature file (staged; apply with changes_commit).
- scenario_update: Replace a scenario's steps and/or requirement tag (staged; apply with changes_commit).
- start_refactor: Begin a refactor step.
- step_definition_create: Stage pending step definitions for every undefined step (template only over MCP; apply with changes_commit).
- step_definitions_find: List Gherkin steps that have no matching step definition.
- unit_test_create: Stage a failing unit test for one requirement (template only over MCP; apply with changes_commit).
- validate_spec: Validate the requirements spec on disk.

